1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
Extruders, molds, injectors.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:10,000
Are these machines manufacturing adhesive, plastics, or parts that go in your car?

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:11,000
No.

4
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:14,000
They're making treats that melt in your mouth, not in your hand.

5
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:22,000
Sit back, relax, and grab a candy bar, potato chip, pretzel lollipop, ice cream cone, or a cupcake

6
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:25,000
while we say a mouthful about some of the world's favorite snacks.

7
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:29,000
Now, Snack Food Tech on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Friars that cook thousands of pounds of sizzling potato chips an hour.

9
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:51,840
Machines that wrap candy faster than the eye can see.

10
00:00:52,380 --> 00:00:55,180
Plants that chill millions of gallons of ice cream a year.

11
00:00:55,181 --> 00:01:00,860
The snack food industry believes in outsized productivity and speed

12
00:01:00,861 --> 00:01:05,680
And it shows no signs of slowing down

13
00:01:05,681 --> 00:01:11,980
I always think machines are so fast, I don't know how we'll ever go faster

14
00:01:11,981 --> 00:01:20,260
And three or four or five years later, we're able to design new machinery that can go faster

15
00:01:20,261 --> 00:01:25,660
It's taken faster machines to keep pace with consumers' demands.

16
00:01:26,660 --> 00:01:32,400
Fifty years ago, snacks were not the grab-and-go product that they are today.

17
00:01:32,780 --> 00:01:35,980
Now they are everywhere in every form that you can imagine.

18
00:01:36,300 --> 00:01:37,480
They fit in your cup holders.

19
00:01:37,940 --> 00:01:39,300
They fit in your lunchbox.

20
00:01:39,500 --> 00:01:40,920
They fit in your sport coat.

21
00:01:41,180 --> 00:01:43,700
They fit wherever you are, whatever you are.

22
00:01:43,701 --> 00:01:50,700
And profits have followed productivity.

23
00:01:51,280 --> 00:02:00,220
In the United States, about $20 to $25 billion worth of confectionery products are sold every year.

24
00:02:00,940 --> 00:02:01,820
That's a lot of candy.

25
00:02:02,440 --> 00:02:07,180
But it takes 24 pounds of candy a year to satisfy the average American sweet tooth.

26
00:02:08,220 --> 00:02:12,800
Mars Incorporated, the private company that makes Snickers, Milky Way and M&M's,

27
00:02:12,801 --> 00:02:16,040
has sales of $16 billion worldwide.

28
00:02:19,260 --> 00:02:21,840
An army of warm nougat and caramel

29
00:02:21,841 --> 00:02:23,700
marches down a conveyor belt.

30
00:02:24,360 --> 00:02:25,720
While hundreds of feet ahead,

31
00:02:26,240 --> 00:02:27,660
a flood of chocolate awaits.

32
00:02:28,580 --> 00:02:30,640
Those bars are actually going to flow

33
00:02:30,641 --> 00:02:33,840
under a waterfall or a curtain of chocolate.

34
00:02:34,280 --> 00:02:36,160
Someplace that we probably would all like to do

35
00:02:36,161 --> 00:02:37,080
sometime in our lives.

36
00:02:38,500 --> 00:02:41,540
Although there's a fantastic aspect to a candy plant

37
00:02:41,541 --> 00:02:44,140
that immediately conjures Charlie and the Chocolate Factory.

38
00:02:44,720 --> 00:02:47,500
What goes on inside is hardly child's play.

39
00:02:48,540 --> 00:02:54,960
Overall in this plant, we make over 30 million pieces of candy a day.

40
00:02:55,560 --> 00:02:56,980
It's a tremendous amount of volume.

41
00:02:57,300 --> 00:03:01,360
That would equate to maybe close to 100,000 metric tons a year of product.

42
00:03:02,520 --> 00:03:04,940
It's impossible to know where it all began,

43
00:03:05,460 --> 00:03:08,860
but certainly humankind's sweet tooth predates recorded history.

44
00:03:08,861 --> 00:03:14,580
The first caveman, when he went out there looking for something tasty and realized that honey was sweet,

45
00:03:14,581 --> 00:03:17,020
then maybe took a stick and poked it in there.

46
00:03:17,340 --> 00:03:20,261
You could say that could be the theoretical origin of lollipops, right?

47
00:03:20,262 --> 00:03:20,962
Honey on a stick.

48
00:03:23,780 --> 00:03:29,580
Ancient Egyptians, Arabs, and Chinese all made confections from nuts and fruits candied in honey.

49
00:03:30,720 --> 00:03:34,780
And by the 17th century, the English were making treats out of boiled sugar.

50
00:03:35,720 --> 00:03:40,200
The other motherlode for the world's sweet tooth was the discovery of chocolate.

51
00:03:43,120 --> 00:03:49,580
Cocoa trees, the source of chocolate, were being cultivated in Mesoamerica several thousand years ago by the Maya.

52
00:03:51,540 --> 00:03:59,060
In 1519, the Aztec ruler Montezuma served Cortes a golden goblet full of a drink called xocolatl,

53
00:03:59,440 --> 00:04:01,580
a spiced concoction of ground cocoa beans.

54
00:04:01,581 --> 00:04:07,040
Cortes returned to Spain with both the drink's recipe and a supply of beans.

55
00:04:08,480 --> 00:04:12,900
Sweetened with cane sugar, the chocolate drink was a hit among European aristocracy.

56
00:04:13,980 --> 00:04:19,520
People started to drink it, and during that time frame, all sorts of weird things were mixed with it.

57
00:04:19,620 --> 00:04:26,620
There's some historical information that people mixed blood with it, they mixed licorice with it, they mixed all sorts of things.

58
00:04:26,621 --> 00:04:32,520
But it would take another 300 years for Europeans to manufacture a creamy, solid chocolate.

59
00:04:33,640 --> 00:04:34,535
In 1828, C.J.

60
00:04:34,536 --> 00:04:40,800
Van Houten, a Dutch chocolate maker, invented a manual cocoa press that made this possible.

61
00:04:42,020 --> 00:04:48,080
At the 1893 World's Fair in Chicago, Milton Hershey purchased German chocolate-making machinery

62
00:04:48,081 --> 00:04:53,020
and began producing chocolate bars in his Lancaster, Pennsylvania factory in 1894.

63
00:04:53,021 --> 00:04:58,020
The mass-produced American chocolate bar was born.

64
00:04:58,021 --> 00:05:03,020
Milton Hershey is credited with kind of creating the candy bar in the United States.

65
00:05:03,021 --> 00:05:07,020
He took milk chocolate and started selling for a nickel apiece.

66
00:05:07,021 --> 00:05:11,020
Since those early days, demand for cocoa has never waned.

67
00:05:11,021 --> 00:05:16,020
Today, it's grown and harvested as it has been for hundreds of years.

68
00:05:16,021 --> 00:05:21,020
This pod will sit right onto a branch, and it'll sit something like that.

69
00:05:21,021 --> 00:05:24,020
And how it's harvested is pretty simplistic.

70
00:05:24,021 --> 00:05:29,020
A machete comes by, chops it right off, pod falls to the ground.

71
00:05:29,021 --> 00:05:33,020
They take that same machete, chop it, split it open.

72
00:05:33,021 --> 00:05:36,020
And inside the pod is something called the placenta.

73
00:05:36,021 --> 00:05:38,020
It's a very wet, moist bean.

74
00:05:38,021 --> 00:05:40,020
It doesn't even look like a bean.

75
00:05:42,020 --> 00:05:44,020
The beans are fermented to bring out their flavor

76
00:05:44,021 --> 00:05:46,020
and then shipped to, among other places,

77
00:05:46,021 --> 00:05:50,020
the U.S. Division of Mars Incorporated, Master Foods USA.

78
00:05:51,020 --> 00:05:58,140
At the factory, the beans are cleaned, roasted, shelled, and ground into a liquid called chocolate liqueur.

79
00:05:59,940 --> 00:06:03,220
Presses separate this liquid into cocoa powder and cocoa butter.

80
00:06:05,160 --> 00:06:09,000
Chocolate liqueur and additional cocoa butter are the main ingredients of chocolate.

81
00:06:10,980 --> 00:06:17,160
Milk solids, sweeteners, and flavors are added to the mix, which is then blended to distribute the cocoa fat in the chocolate.

82
00:06:17,161 --> 00:06:23,040
This process is much the same today as it was in 1923

83
00:06:23,041 --> 00:06:26,540
When Frank C. Mars introduced the Milky Way

84
00:06:26,541 --> 00:06:28,640
The nation's first filled candy bar

85
00:06:28,641 --> 00:06:32,300
Mars was a successful candy salesman

86
00:06:32,301 --> 00:06:34,760
Who had started his business with his wife, Ethel

87
00:06:34,761 --> 00:06:36,680
In their Tacoma, Washington kitchen

88
00:06:36,681 --> 00:06:41,100
But it was the popularity of the chocolate malted milkshake

89
00:06:41,101 --> 00:06:44,640
That gave Mars the idea of creating a portable bite-sized version

90
00:06:44,641 --> 00:06:49,640
The Milky Way bar was an overnight sensation.

91
00:06:49,641 --> 00:06:51,640
Mars Incorporated was born.

92
00:06:51,641 --> 00:06:56,640
But Frank Mars needed a large plant to keep pace with the demand for his new product.

93
00:06:56,641 --> 00:07:02,640
The reason he chose Chicago was because of its national distribution potential, rail access in and out,

94
00:07:02,641 --> 00:07:07,640
bringing raw materials in to make the product, as well as being able to ship the product out by rail.

95
00:07:07,641 --> 00:07:13,460
The history of this particular plant is that we really began production in 1929

96
00:07:13,461 --> 00:07:17,260
with the Milky Way bar shortly followed in 1930 by the Snickers

97
00:07:17,261 --> 00:07:20,240
and then the Three Musketeers bars in 1932.

98
00:07:21,180 --> 00:07:24,720
At the plant, candy manufacture, though scaled up,

99
00:07:24,860 --> 00:07:27,280
was still a handmade single-batch process.

100
00:07:27,780 --> 00:07:29,560
If you look at the Chicago factory,

101
00:07:29,840 --> 00:07:32,520
and if you look at what it would have been like in the 1930s,

102
00:07:32,820 --> 00:07:34,880
it's much like what your kitchen is today,

103
00:07:35,400 --> 00:07:37,040
except on a larger scale.

104
00:07:37,041 --> 00:07:44,340
Those were mechanical items physically done by hand, which would require someone to mix it first.

105
00:07:44,820 --> 00:07:48,200
Then they would have to put the product on a slab, lay that out by hand.

106
00:07:48,560 --> 00:07:53,000
They literally would then take a cutter and cut the product by hand.

107
00:07:53,300 --> 00:08:01,340
So the continuous processing was the big change that really was the leap forward in being able to mass produce the candy products.

108
00:08:01,341 --> 00:08:12,280
Continuous processing, a series of machines in sequence that move product from mixing through wrapping without stopping, came to the plant in 1947.

109
00:08:13,360 --> 00:08:22,320
Today, this process is still used to make all Master Foods products, from M&M's to Milky Way to these Snickers bars, named after the Mars family's favorite horse.

110
00:08:22,321 --> 00:08:30,880
The first process is really the whipping of egg white along with some cooked corn syrup,

111
00:08:30,881 --> 00:08:38,280
which includes some salt and some water and sugar because we want a high impact of peanut

112
00:08:38,281 --> 00:08:39,280
flavor in Snickers.

113
00:08:39,281 --> 00:08:43,200
We're adding fresh ground peanut butter to that mixture.

114
00:08:43,201 --> 00:08:46,520
We can't have a great Snickers bar without having great caramel.

115
00:08:46,521 --> 00:08:52,120
And it comes into this area, into the blender, where it's now being blended with our own

116
00:08:52,121 --> 00:08:53,220
fresh roasted peanuts.

117
00:08:54,740 --> 00:08:57,620
Although the process may look simple, it's not.

118
00:08:58,280 --> 00:09:01,680
Time and temperature are very, very critical components.

119
00:09:02,280 --> 00:09:07,620
And for us, monitoring those closely, as well as even basic things like humidity, which

120
00:09:07,621 --> 00:09:12,020
could cause the candy to become very sticky and difficult to handle, are all very critical.

121
00:09:12,021 --> 00:09:18,600
For a line like this that has a capacity of making 50 or 60 tons of product to shift,

122
00:09:18,980 --> 00:09:24,680
you can imagine if one of those parameters would get out of control, you'd have quite a sticky mess in your hands.

123
00:09:26,760 --> 00:09:31,560
The 195-degree nougat flows downstairs and into a hopper.

124
00:09:31,860 --> 00:09:35,220
It's then squeezed between two ice-cold stainless steel rollers.

125
00:09:35,221 --> 00:09:44,180
And the material spreads out into a slab that then is peeled off of those rollers and laid down onto a stainless steel belt to be further cooled.

126
00:09:44,540 --> 00:09:47,680
And then head towards the area where we can actually put caramel on top.

127
00:09:48,960 --> 00:09:59,080
The combined slab will be cooled further to 65 degrees Fahrenheit so that it can be cut cleanly into strips the exact width of a Snickers bar without chocolate on it.

128
00:10:00,280 --> 00:10:03,920
The caramel and nougat slab is now warm for enrobing.

129
00:10:03,921 --> 00:10:07,920
That's industry speak for getting its chocolate covering.

130
00:10:07,921 --> 00:10:12,920
The chocolate has been tempered, heated and cooled in a way that ensures a more uniform

131
00:10:12,921 --> 00:10:17,920
crystallization of cocoa butter within the chocolate, resulting in a higher melting point.

132
00:10:17,921 --> 00:10:22,920
Which makes that chocolate easier to handle in your hands at room temperature and at body temperature.

133
00:10:22,921 --> 00:10:28,920
Really what we want is we want that chocolate to melt in your mouth, but not in your hands.

134
00:10:28,921 --> 00:10:33,960
Then the bars are vibrated to rid them of air bubbles and to evenly distribute the chocolate.

135
00:10:34,980 --> 00:10:38,160
The tempering process continues as the bars head toward wrapping.

136
00:10:39,960 --> 00:10:46,860
The Snickers bars receive a final inspection before being wrapped at rates of between 800 and 900 bars per minute.

137
00:10:47,720 --> 00:10:52,100
The results of all this work for Master Foods is a spectacular payoff.

138
00:10:52,101 --> 00:10:59,100
The Snicker bar, while invented in 1930 today, is one of the top selling confectionery brands in the world.

139
00:10:59,101 --> 00:11:00,700
Two billion in global sales.

140
00:11:03,400 --> 00:11:13,580
But Snickers is only one of the company's many brands, which include M&Ms, Twix, Starburst, Skittles, Milky Way, Kudos, and Three Musketeers.

141
00:11:14,980 --> 00:11:19,500
At the Chicago plant, they also make the newest product, the Amazing Bar.

142
00:11:19,501 --> 00:11:23,080
a molded milk chocolate bar with embedded M&M's minis.

143
00:11:26,060 --> 00:11:29,680
And Poppables, candy bars in a bite-sized format.

144
00:11:30,740 --> 00:11:34,460
The company is privately held and run by the fourth generation of the Mars family.

145
00:11:34,860 --> 00:11:36,980
With products sold in over 100 countries,

146
00:11:37,540 --> 00:11:39,980
they've come a long way from Frank and Ethel Mars' kitchen.

147
00:11:45,920 --> 00:11:49,480
Creativity in the kitchen was also the genesis of this popular snack.

148
00:11:49,500 --> 00:11:52,920
Potato chips are made from special potatoes

149
00:11:52,921 --> 00:11:56,880
And more of them are grown here in this potato chip powerhouse state

150
00:11:56,881 --> 00:11:59,040
Than anywhere else

151
00:11:59,041 --> 00:12:01,400
And no, it's not Idaho

152
00:12:01,401 --> 00:12:07,980
The melting point of cocoa butter between 97 and 99 degrees Fahrenheit

153
00:12:07,981 --> 00:12:10,320
Is right at human body temperature

154
00:12:10,321 --> 00:12:12,900
Which is why chocolate melts in your mouth

155
00:12:12,901 --> 00:12:15,980
Snack food tech will return on Modern Marvels

156
00:12:15,981 --> 00:12:21,360
We now return to Snack Food Tech on Modern Marvels.

157
00:12:24,220 --> 00:12:28,720
Americans eat $24 billion worth of savory snacks a year.

158
00:12:29,120 --> 00:12:33,760
That's loads of potato chips, corn chips, pretzels, and just about anything you can put salt on.

159
00:12:35,200 --> 00:12:41,380
Nine of the ten top-selling brands are made by one company, Frito-Lay, the biggest player in the industry.

160
00:12:42,260 --> 00:12:45,060
Even though you have one large national player,

161
00:12:45,400 --> 00:12:50,660
you do have dozens of strong, mostly family-owned regional companies.

162
00:12:53,440 --> 00:12:56,200
Pennsylvania, the chipping potato state.

163
00:12:56,980 --> 00:12:59,200
That moniker probably won't replace the state motto,

164
00:12:59,420 --> 00:13:02,760
but the area around Hanover and York in southeastern Pennsylvania

165
00:13:02,761 --> 00:13:05,080
is nicknamed the potato chip belt.

166
00:13:05,500 --> 00:13:06,520
For a reason.

167
00:13:06,521 --> 00:13:13,620
Not just any old potato is fit to become a potato chip.

168
00:13:15,100 --> 00:13:20,420
They're bred specifically for that purpose, to be round and to have low moisture content.

169
00:13:21,120 --> 00:13:24,940
Water has to be cooked out, and too much of it leads to a bland-tasting chip.

170
00:13:26,900 --> 00:13:31,720
Although the potato was discovered and cultivated by pre-Columbian farmers in the Andes Mountains,

171
00:13:32,640 --> 00:13:36,720
It took several thousand years for someone to stumble on the idea for the potato chip.

172
00:13:38,380 --> 00:13:45,440
In 1853, George Crumb was working as a chef at the upscale Moon Lake Lodge in Saratoga Springs, New York.

173
00:13:46,760 --> 00:13:52,180
The quick-tempered chef was insulted when a patron returned some French fries because they were too thick.

174
00:13:52,520 --> 00:13:58,720
In response, Crumb cut the potatoes thin as paper, fried them to a crisp, and sent them back to the table.

175
00:13:59,540 --> 00:14:01,340
Of course, the chips were a hit.

176
00:14:01,720 --> 00:14:05,375
He created a brand new product. There was no R&D involved with it.

177
00:14:05,376 --> 00:14:06,760
It was totally impulsive.

178
00:14:07,260 --> 00:14:13,860
The ingredients involved with this was anger and maybe a little bit of exacting of revenge.

179
00:14:14,540 --> 00:14:19,320
By the 20th century, people began frying chips in their kitchens and selling them locally.

180
00:14:20,020 --> 00:14:22,640
This was especially prevalent in southeastern Pennsylvania.

181
00:14:23,620 --> 00:14:30,160
They tended to have the entrepreneurial spirit, independent, some people would say hard-headed,

182
00:14:30,161 --> 00:14:34,100
but they'd like to be on their own and be self-sufficient.

183
00:14:34,560 --> 00:14:39,320
They were growing potatoes, plus the capital expenditure to get into the business

184
00:14:39,321 --> 00:14:45,580
with those early, very simple equipment and production methods was low cost and easy.

185
00:14:46,380 --> 00:14:51,360
In 1921, Butts Quality Foods in Hanover, Pennsylvania was launched

186
00:14:51,361 --> 00:14:54,420
with this combination of modest expense and hard work.

187
00:14:54,421 --> 00:15:01,840
My grandfather, William Butts, was a factory worker at Hanover Shoe in Hanover,

188
00:15:02,420 --> 00:15:05,480
and Sally, his wife, was a homemaker.

189
00:15:06,560 --> 00:15:10,560
Bill heard about an older gentleman in York, Pennsylvania,

190
00:15:10,740 --> 00:15:14,240
who had a small chip kettle that wanted to get out of the business,

191
00:15:14,241 --> 00:15:18,560
so he quit his job in Hanover Shoe, scraped the other $300,

192
00:15:18,561 --> 00:15:24,400
bought the equipment and brought it to their home in downtown Hanover.

193
00:15:24,820 --> 00:15:29,160
So from that point, Grandma Salie began cooking the chips,

194
00:15:29,380 --> 00:15:32,620
and Bill would go door to door selling them.

195
00:15:33,480 --> 00:15:38,080
By 1930, Hanover home brand potato chip, as the company was called at the time,

196
00:15:38,500 --> 00:15:40,420
expanded to a building behind the house.

197
00:15:41,280 --> 00:15:46,320
By 1936, their automatic potato chip cooker was frying 300 pounds of chips an hour.

198
00:15:47,160 --> 00:15:49,640
His early cooker was a quantum leap forward.

199
00:15:51,020 --> 00:15:55,720
But nothing compared to this contemporary $5.2 million fryer.

200
00:15:57,540 --> 00:16:01,000
We're now at the beginning of one of our continuous fryers.

201
00:16:01,080 --> 00:16:02,240
This is our biggest fryer.

202
00:16:02,800 --> 00:16:06,980
It's capable of doing 4,200 pounds of chips in an hour.

203
00:16:07,940 --> 00:16:11,100
Before the chips hit the oil, the potatoes must be sorted.

204
00:16:11,101 --> 00:16:20,580
One of the things a lot of people don't realize is we have to sort the potatoes into various sizes in order to get those potatoes in the bags.

205
00:16:20,960 --> 00:16:24,960
You use big potatoes for big bags and small potatoes for small bags.

206
00:16:25,840 --> 00:16:33,480
These potatoes, a variety called Snowdens, are pumped into the plant in a flume of water that efficiently washes them as it moves them.

207
00:16:34,480 --> 00:16:37,180
It takes four pounds of potatoes to make a pound of chips.

208
00:16:37,181 --> 00:16:39,248
This is a modern peeler.

209
00:16:39,249 --> 00:16:46,140
There are a series of drums where we can adjust the abrasive inside those drums.

210
00:16:46,780 --> 00:16:53,860
Depending on the kind of potato and the toughness of the skin, we will literally rub the outside off.

211
00:16:54,780 --> 00:16:57,820
The peels aren't wasted. They'll be sold for animal feed.

212
00:16:59,200 --> 00:17:01,680
Once peeled, the potatoes are off to the slicer.

213
00:17:01,681 --> 00:17:05,680
a spinning ring of surgical steel blades set with a micrometer,

214
00:17:06,300 --> 00:17:12,960
to a slice thickness of between .045 and .065 inches for their various chips.

215
00:17:14,840 --> 00:17:17,180
After excess moisture is blown off,

216
00:17:17,560 --> 00:17:22,440
they're carried into a bath in 2,800 gallons of 350-degree cottonseed oil.

217
00:17:23,460 --> 00:17:27,880
There are actually hole-down belts that are inside this continuous fryer

218
00:17:27,881 --> 00:17:31,120
that push the slices down into the oil,

219
00:17:31,121 --> 00:17:38,780
and then there are paddles on those belts so that they bring those slices along and turn them into chips as they come through the hot oil.

220
00:17:40,180 --> 00:17:44,200
Salt is added to the chips as they come out of the fryer, and they're immediately inspected.

221
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:54,580
Right after the chips are salted, they go through a machine that actually sorts out the dark chips or the chips that have blemishes.

222
00:17:54,581 --> 00:18:02,580
This machine has cameras in it and is set so that when it detects any kind of blemish

223
00:18:02,581 --> 00:18:08,580
or dark spot, it hits that chip with a puff of air and puts it down into a channel to

224
00:18:08,581 --> 00:18:13,580
have it removed from the stream of chips.

225
00:18:13,581 --> 00:18:18,580
Pickers will then inspect the chips again before they fall into buckets on the weigh scales.

226
00:18:18,581 --> 00:18:21,700
The weight in each bucket is registered on a computer,

227
00:18:22,420 --> 00:18:25,840
and then the exact combination of buckets are opened up

228
00:18:25,841 --> 00:18:30,740
to get the weight in the bag within one gram of what is called for.

229
00:18:32,120 --> 00:18:35,380
The bags are flushed with nitrogen before the chips go in.

230
00:18:36,460 --> 00:18:40,160
They'll last five weeks longer in a nitrogen environment than they would in oxygen,

231
00:18:40,680 --> 00:18:42,200
up to eight weeks on the shelf.

232
00:18:42,201 --> 00:18:54,240
Utz makes one million pounds of potato chips a week in over 100 varieties, including old-fashioned hand-cooked chips.

233
00:18:54,600 --> 00:19:02,620
As far as hand-cooked potato chips, we have a broader range of varieties than anybody in the industry

234
00:19:02,621 --> 00:19:10,920
